156:{"name": "individual_computed_rule_search_effort", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `effortful_computed_rule_search`: people search for arithmetic rules that have to be computed (primes, squares and cubes, powers, multiples other than 5) only on some judgments, and otherwise generalize from the explanations visible at a glance (ranges, odd or even, digits, multiples of 5), weighing whichever concepts they considered by the same Bayesian logic. The single change is that how often a person invests this effort is a stable personal trait rather than a population constant (each participant's propensity to search differs from the population's by a fixed spread, inferred from their answers). This predicts that some participants consistently extend computed rules to distant members (e.g. 16, 8, 64 → 32, 2) across every rule-fitting set they see, while others consistently answer such sets by range and digit pattern, with no individual differences on sets that no computed rule fits.", "context": "experiment3 round 3 candidate 3 refine incumbent effortful_computed_rule_search"}
157:{"name": "individual_computed_rule_search_effort_2", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "predicts like existing model 'individual_computed_rule_search_effort' (p_yes RMSE 0.00000 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of individual_computed_rule_search_effort, not a new hypothesis.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `effortful_computed_rule_search`: people only sometimes make the deliberate effort to consider computed arithmetic rules (primes, squares and cubes, powers, multiples other than 5), and otherwise weigh only the at-a-glance concepts (ranges, parity, digit patterns, multiples of 5) by the same Bayesian logic. The single change is that how often a person makes this effort is a stable trait of that person rather than a population constant: each participant has their own search propensity, spread around the population by a fixed amount and inferred from their answers. So the same participants consistently extend computed rules to distant members (and reject in-span non-members) across every set they see, while others consistently fall back on ranges and digit patterns, and this split shows only for sets that a computed rule explains.", "context": "experiment3 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent effortful_computed_rule_search"}
158-{"name": "loose_ranges_effortful_rule_search", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `individual_range_sampling_looseness`: people do the same capacity-limited Bayesian learning over arithmetic rules (kind plausibilities, sparse familiarities within kinds) and numerical ranges. They read the examples more loosely for ranges than for rules, and how loosely they read them as range evidence is a stable trait of each participant. The single change, a component taken from `effortful_computed_rule_search`: considering rules that have to be computed (primes, squares and cubes, powers, multiples other than 5) takes deliberate effort, so people search for them only on a fixed share of judgments. On the rest they weigh only the explanations visible at a glance (stretches of the number line, odd or even, final or contained digits, multiples of 5). So even sets that fit a computed rule well (e.g. 16, 8, 64) get a share of range-based answers, and on those answers each person's own range looseness decides how far beyond the examples' span their yeses reach.", "context": "experiment3 round 3 candidate 5 refine chosen"}
159-{"name": "individual_response_decisiveness", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People infer the hidden concept by the same capacity-limited Bayesian learning over arithmetic rules (kind plausibilities, a few exceptionally familiar or unfamiliar rules) and numerical ranges as the simplest current model, but they differ stably in how decisively they turn that belief into a yes/no answer: some participants answer more categorically than their belief (near-unanimous yes to well-supported targets and no to poorly supported ones), others more hesitantly, closer to a coin flip. Unlike a yes-bias, this personal decisiveness leaves a 50/50 belief untouched and pushes answers away from or toward chance symmetrically, so a participant's distinctiveness shows up on confidently judged targets (examples, far non-members) and points the same way across every set they see.", "context": "experiment3 round 3 candidate 2 lens 8 repair 1"}
160-{"name": "individual_leaning_effortful_rule_search", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `effortful_computed_rule_search`: people search for arithmetic rules that have to be computed (primes, squares and cubes, powers, multiples other than 5) only on a fixed share of judgments, and otherwise weigh only the explanations visible at a glance (ranges, odd or even, digits, multiples of 5) by the same Bayesian logic. The single change, a component taken from `loose_range_individual_rule_leaning`, is that participants also differ stably in how readily they reach for any rule rather than a stretch of the number line: each person's leaning shifts the prior odds of every rule (computed or glance) against the ranges, with a fixed spread around the population. This predicts individual differences that appear even for sets no computed rule fits (e.g. all-even or shared-final-digit sets), where range-leaning people still give yeses to in-span non-members and rule-leaning people to distant same-parity or same-digit targets, consistently across every set each person sees.", "context": "experiment3 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent effortful_computed_rule_search repair 1"}
161:{"name": "individual_computed_rule_search_effort", "outcome": "dropped", "detail": "fold 2 too slow to fit: the fit of 'individual_computed_rule_search_effort' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `effortful_computed_rule_search`: people search for arithmetic rules that have to be computed (primes, squares and cubes, powers, multiples other than 5) only on some judgments, and otherwise generalize from the explanations visible at a glance (ranges, odd or even, digits, multiples of 5), weighing whichever concepts they considered by the same Bayesian logic. The single change is that how often a person invests this effort is a stable personal trait rather than a population constant (each participant's propensity to search differs from the population's by a fixed spread, inferred from their answers). This predicts that some participants consistently extend computed rules to distant members (e.g. 16, 8, 64 → 32, 2) across every rule-fitting set they see, while others consistently answer such sets by range and digit pattern, with no individual differences on sets that no computed rule fits.", "context": "experiment3 round 3"}
